// Java equivalent of SequentialVsConcurrent.kt: blocking calls on virtual threads,
// started together with CompletableFuture and joined afterwards.
package workbench.coroutines.l02;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class SequentialVsConcurrentJava {
    static String quote(String provider, long latencyMs) {
        try { Thread.sleep(latencyMs); } catch (InterruptedException e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); } // a network call
        return provider + ":" + latencyMs + "ms";
    }

    static final List<Map.Entry<String, Long>> LATENCIES =
        List.of(Map.entry("northbank", 300L), Map.entry("fjord-fx", 120L), Map.entry("tidewater", 200L));

    static List<String> sequential() {
        return LATENCIES.stream().map(e -> quote(e.getKey(), e.getValue())).toList();
    }

    static List<String> concurrent(ExecutorService vthreads) {
        List<CompletableFuture<String>> pending = LATENCIES.stream()
            .map(e -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> quote(e.getKey(), e.getValue()), vthreads)) // start all
            .toList();
        return pending.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList();                                // then wait
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (ExecutorService vthreads = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            long t0 = System.nanoTime();
            List<String> r1 = sequential();
            System.out.printf("sequential: %s in %d ms%n", r1, (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000);
            long t1 = System.nanoTime();
            List<String> r2 = concurrent(vthreads);
            System.out.printf("concurrent: %s in %d ms%n", r2, (System.nanoTime() - t1) / 1_000_000);
        }
    }
}

sections.body:
`coroutineScope` is how a suspend function gets a scope to start children in. It creates a scope, runs the block, and then suspends until every coroutine started inside has finished. Only then does it return the block's value.

```kotlin
suspend fun warmUp() = coroutineScope {
    providers.forEach { p -> launch { p.quote("EURUSD") } }
}   // returns after the slowest provider has answered
```

```java
// Java equivalent: an executor's close() waits for every task it accepted (JDK 19+)
void warmUp() {
    try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
        providers.forEach(p -> executor.submit(() -> p.quote("EURUSD")));
    }   // returns after the slowest provider has answered
}
```

That gives your callers a strong promise: when a suspend function returns, the work it started is done. Nothing keeps running behind their back, holding a connection or writing to the ledger after the request has finished.

Failure travels the same path in reverse. If one child fails, `coroutineScope` cancels the other children, waits for them to stop, and rethrows the exception to its caller. In `allQuotes`, a provider that throws at 50 ms makes the whole call fail at about 50 ms, and the two other requests are cancelled instead of left running. Lesson 3 of this track draws this tree of parents and children, and lesson 6 of this track is about the exceptions.

`coroutineScope` suspends; it does not block. The thread is free while it waits. It is itself a suspend function, so you call it from suspend code, and you can nest it as deep as you like. The lab's `quoteBoard` puts `allQuotes` inside another `coroutineScope`: two currency pairs times three providers is six calls in flight at once, and the board is still ready in 300 ms.

The builders so far, side by side: `launch` and `async` start a child and return at once, while `coroutineScope` runs a block and suspends until all of its children are done. You will rarely write `launch` or `async` without a `coroutineScope` (or a class-owned scope, lesson 3 of this track) around them.

